<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE suite SYSTEM "https://testng.org/testng-1.0.dtd">
<!--
    MODULE-SCOPED SUITE - Agency. See workflows_and_pipelines/pipeline/pipeline.md §12.9.

    Generated by run-004 (US-4222 Update Agency), Stage 4A. Lets Stage 5A and Stage 5H run the
    Agency module on its own, without touching the Branch module's tests.

        mvn test -DsuiteXmlFile=testng-agency.xml     module-scoped run (Stage 5A, 5H reruns)

    ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    WHY THIS FILE HAS ONE <test> BLOCK AND testng-branch.xml HAS FOUR

    This is not an inconsistency to tidy up. TestNG scopes @Test(priority = ...) to the enclosing
    <test> tag, NOT to the suite. Splitting the Agency classes into one <test> per class - the shape
    testng-branch.xml uses - would make each class's priorities independent, and the suite's required
    cross-class execution order would silently stop being honoured.

    Agency needs that order because Automation Plan §5.1 gives the whole suite ONE shared Agency
    record, on the operator's instruction. The tests are therefore not mutually independent:

        priority   5-6     read-only inheritance and read-only assertions, before anything mutates
        priority  10-50    field persistence
        priority  60-80    validation (restores every field it clears)
        priority  90-120   documents and logo
        priority 200-230   maker-checker LAST: it drives the record through
                           submit -> reject -> resubmit -> approve, which are one-way state
                           transitions on the single shared record, not repeatable actions

    Branch has no such constraint - each of its classes provisions its own record - which is why the
    two files legitimately differ in shape.

    preserve-order="true" keeps declaration order as the tie-break; priority is what actually
    sequences the run.
-->
<suite name="AgencyModuleSuite" verbose="1">
    <test name="AgencyUpdateSuite" preserve-order="true">
        <classes>
            <!-- Read-only first: neither case saves anything, so both observe a pristine fixture. -->
            <class name="AdminPages.Admin.Agency.AgencyInheritanceAndReadOnly_TC"/>
            <class name="AdminPages.Admin.Agency.AgencyUpdatePersistence_TC"/>
            <class name="AdminPages.Admin.Agency.AgencyUpdateValidation_TC"/>
            <class name="AdminPages.Admin.Agency.AgencyDocumentsAndLogo_TC"/>
            <!-- Last: leaves the fixture approved, with test values applied. -->
            <class name="AdminPages.Admin.Agency.AgencyMakerChecker_TC"/>
        </classes>
    </test>
</suite>
